{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"WAVE 2 - THE FLOOR MECHANISM, read from source. rising_floor_drop at src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/decide.py:105-119 drops iff novelty>=0.7 and priority<0.4, applied by _apply_floor_to_verdict in llm/plan_review/floors.py:24-57, gated by _maybe_apply_rising_floor in llm/plan_review/__init__.py:114-165. IT SUPPRESSES THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT WAS ASSUMED: it drops NOVEL LOW-PRIORITY findings, the noise an edit stirs up. A prior-round blocking finding is a carryover with novelty~0 and is EXPLICITLY KEPT - there is no 'already-seen gets suppressed' behaviour, and the predicate exists precisely to protect that case. So 'rebar re-litigates its own remediated findings' was a wrong framing on my part and is retracted. Jurisdiction is ONE bucket: floors.py:36 reads verdict['advisory']; blocking, indeterminate and overflow are never iterated. That explains 2948-48e5-PASS, where all 5 preconditions held, the floor path RAN, and it floored nothing because 25 of 27 findings were indeterminate. Preconditions (remediation_mode.py:33, all must hold): plan fingerprint CHANGED, verified_at_sha UNCHANGED, regver unchanged, prior sidecar present, within 60 minutes. Signed prior NOT required. No config flag - the off switch was retired in story 4cdf, so it is on by default."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789000911805117001,"uuid":"20d9563c-3882-4c22-bdb4-8e655eaf412d"}